"A Buddhist reader recognizes a great deal in the cave. There is a condition of bondage that the bound do not notice, which the tradition calls avidyā, ignorance. There is a path of gradual seeing. And there is the return. The one who has seen and goes back among the prisoners out of pity, expecting to be mocked, is very close to the Mahāyāna ideal of the bodhisattva, who declines to rest in his own liberation while others remain bound. The Lotus Sūtra tells a comparable story of a father who coaxes his children out of a burning house in which they are happily playing.": "e7e42f07b93a",
"As a rule of thumb, seeing is believing is a good one.": "ab15fcfbbcab",
"Before he leaves the image, Socrates draws a conclusion about teaching that every teacher should know. Education is not what some people say it is, putting knowledge into a soul that lacks it, like sight into blind eyes. The power to learn exists in the soul already. What is needed is a turning around: just as the eye was unable to turn from darkness to light without the whole body, so too the instrument of knowledge can only by the movement of the whole soul be turned from the world of becoming into that of being. On this view a teacher transmits nothing. The teachers work is to arrange things so that the student turns and looks. The turning is uncomfortable, and the student may well resent the person who causes it.": "3423bb382d5f",
"Few philosophers now accept Platos Forms. His own pupil Aristotle argued that the form of a thing exists in the thing and cannot be found in a separate realm. But the cave has outlived the theory it was built to illustrate, because the situation it describes keeps recurring. People do mistake the images of things for things. Much of what anyone believes about the wider world was seen on a screen, arranged by people who remain out of sight behind a wall. And the story makes a point about method that does not depend on any theory of Forms. From inside the cave, the shadows are clear and the truth is a painful blur. So the vividness of an experience is no measure of its reality. Sight is good evidence, in other words, and it still has to answer to understanding.": "edc4e8a3c23d",
"Form": "3eb178b15595",
"His eyes adjust by stages.": "4528778f45c1",
"Justice itself, as opposed to this or that just act.": "34f2e01d53ac",
"Now suppose one prisoner is released and forced to stand up and turn around. The movement hurts, and the fire dazzles him. Told that what he sees now is more real than the shadows, he does not believe it, for the shadows were clear and these shapes are a blur. If he is then dragged up the steep, rough passage into the sunlight, he is at first unable to see anything at all. His eyes adjust by stages. He can look first at shadows, then at reflections in water, then at things themselves, then at the moon and stars by night. Last of all he will be able to see the sun, and not mere reflections of him in the water. He comes to understand that the sun produces the seasons and governs everything in the visible world, and that it is in some way the cause of all that he and his companions used to see.": "90becdffb7c1",
"On what is seen outside, the two part completely. Platos freed prisoner finds permanent, self-standing essences, and they are more real than anything in the cave. The Madhyamaka philosophers taught two truths, conventional and ultimate, which sounds like the same division. But their ultimate truth is not a second world of better objects. It is the emptiness of the things of this world: the fact that nothing, anywhere, has the kind of independent, unchanging nature that a Platonic Form is supposed to have. For Nāgārjuna, the belief in fixed essences is the chain. On his view the prisoner turns around and finds no sun. What he comes to see is that even the fire and the wall arise in dependence on conditions, and this is the insight that frees him (Dharma and the West, D10).": "1084ab989fd1",
"Picture people living in an underground cave whose long entrance is open to the daylight. Here they have been from their childhood, and have their legs and necks chained so that they cannot move, and can only see before them. Behind and above them a fire is burning. Between the fire and the prisoners runs a raised path with a low wall along it, like the screen at a puppet show, and along the path people carry statues and figures of animals, which stick up above the wall. The firelight throws the shadows of these figures on the wall of the cave in front of the prisoners.": "0ec445ed87d6",
"Seeing is believing is a proverb about evidence, and as a rule of thumb it is a good one. Yet the things we see are a strange guide to how the world is. The sun looks about the size of a coin and appears to cross the sky. A straight stick looks bent in water. The night sky shows stars that may have burned out long ago. In each case we have learned to correct what we see by what we understand. Plato asked how far that correction might go. Suppose that everything the senses show us is related to reality in the way that a picture of a thing is related to the thing. He put the thought in the form of a story, told by Socrates in Book VII of the Republic, and it has become the best-known image in philosophy.": "a6a15bee06da",
"Sight is good evidence, and it still has to answer to understanding.": "9ac41f831036",
"Socrates explains the image himself. The prison-house is the world of sight, the light of the fire is the sun, and the journey upward is the ascent of the soul into the intellectual world. So the cave is the whole world that our senses reveal, and the world outside stands for what can be grasped only by thinking. The things out there are what Plato calls Forms: justice itself, beauty itself, the triangle that geometry studies, as opposed to this or that just act, beautiful face, or chalk drawing. The sun stands for the highest of them, the Form of the Good, which, he says, appears last of all, and is seen only with an effort.": "07a51e27cc73",
"The cave has outlived the theory it was built to illustrate.": "4505babdaea8",
"The movement hurts, and the fire dazzles him.": "67ca53e81b5e",
"The prisoners have never seen anything else. They give the shadows names, and they honor whoever is quickest at predicting which shadow will come next. If the carriers speak, the echo seems to come from the shadows. To them, Socrates says, the truth would be literally nothing but the shadows of the images. His companion remarks that these are strange prisoners. Socrates answers: Like ourselves.": "0ee7cbcf4c18",
"The return is also a duty. In Platos ideal city, those who have seen the Good are not allowed to stay above. They must be made to descend again among the prisoners in the den, and partake of their labours and honours. They would prefer to stay in the light, and Plato argues that this is exactly what fits them to govern.": "bdcc5d497b95",
"The situation it describes keeps recurring.": "b6443e8cbbdd",
"The story does not end in the sunlight. The freed prisoner remembers the others and pities them, and he goes back down. His eyes, used to the sun, are now useless in the dark. He does badly at the shadow contests. Men would say of him that up he went and down he came without his eyes, and that the journey is not worth making. If anyone tried to free them and lead them up, Socrates adds, they would kill him if they could. Platos first readers knew whom he meant. Athens had put Socrates to death some twenty years before.": "e74c8840dce6",
"The student may well resent the person who causes the turning.": "37285ff7913f",
"The world outside stands for what can be grasped only by thinking.": "19316b3b3060",
"To them, I said, the truth would be literally nothing but the shadows of the images.": "908a626714b7",
"We know equality well enough to see that the sticks fall short.": "dae6b158030e",
"Why believe in such things? Platos main reason concerns knowledge. Whatever we perceive is changing, and it has its qualities only more or less: two sticks are equal from here and unequal from there, and a law is just in one city and unjust in another. Yet we do seem to know what equality and justice are, well enough to see that the sticks and the laws fall short. Plato concludes that knowledge must have objects of its own, stable and perfect, which particular things only resemble.": "4b8f478deaf4",
